WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:15.240
- So it's really terrific to
be able to welcome everybody

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:16.820
to this event.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:19.340
This is The Fourth Annual Symposium

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:22.560
on Hybrid Scholarship at Yale University.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.570
We call this event Beyond Boundaries,

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:26.360
this is the fourth year in a row

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:28.210
that this event has been organized

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:30.540
as part of the program of the

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.240
Digital Humanities Laboratory

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:33.950
here at Yale University Library,

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:36.110
and the fourth year it's been organized

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:39.570
almost single handedly by Catherine DeRose

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.670
whom I hope most of you
in the room have heard of

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:42.890
if not met.

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:44.560
Cathy is in a new role here

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:46.250
in the Digital Humanities Laboratory

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:50.000
serving as the manager for
the Digital Humanities Lab,

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.800
and it's really due to her
organization and her vision

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.246
that we've been able to do these

00:00:54.246 --> 00:00:57.703
fourth year in a row of the
Beyond Boundaries symposium.

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:02.180
And really the move to
this part of the library

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:04.210
because those of you
who've attended before

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:06.220
know that we've been in a lecture hall.

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:07.980
The move to this part of the library

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.030
and the connection with the Franke Family

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:12.260
Digital Humanities Lab to my right

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:14.990
is symbolic of a bigger renovation

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:17.490
a bigger shift in the library physically

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:19.940
and a really exciting
suite of new services

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:22.600
that we are offering
in the new Franke Room.

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:24.520
To support Digital Scholarship,

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:26.710
Hybrid Scholarship and
the Digital Humanities

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:28.620
in a beautiful new room.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.720
Through the generosity
of the Franke Family

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.360
in conjunction with
the Goizueta Foundation

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.210
we're gonna be holding our Poster Session

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:39.050
at the end of today's
activities in the Franke Room

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:41.720
which I hope those of you in
the room have seen this before

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:42.553
but if you haven't

00:01:42.553 --> 00:01:44.750
it's a really terrific opportunity to see

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:46.370
the breadth and depth of projects

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:48.610
that we're highlighting in the Franke Room

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:51.133
right after our round
table discussion today.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:53.700
As I mentioned this is the fourth year

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:55.140
we've held Beyond Boundaries

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:57.530
and we're doing it in partnership

00:01:57.530 --> 00:01:59.080
not only with Yale STEAM,

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:01.880
which is a group that is
dedicated to the intersection of

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:03.960
STEM and the Arts and Humanities

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:04.980
but also this year

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:06.770
in a new effort with Code for Good,

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:09.040
which is a group on campus
that is interested in

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:12.200
the social impact of code and programming.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:14.860
We're really excited to be
able to partner with these two

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:16.480
student focused organizations

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:18.173
to bring you Beyond Boundaries.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.800
We're gonna just go right
in to our lighting talks,

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:24.190
I think we have seven folks

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:26.400
who are going to be able to
give us some terrific insight

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:28.090
into things they've been working on.

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:30.060
These include efforts by undergraduates

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:31.850
and graduate students

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:34.036
and so without further ado

00:02:34.036 --> 00:02:35.887
I would like to ask Zen to come up

00:02:35.887 --> 00:02:37.530
and to talk to us about

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:39.410
Organizing without Oppression

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:42.010
Building Democracies in the 21st Century.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:42.843
Zen.

00:02:43.758 --> 00:02:46.008
(applause)

00:02:58.730 --> 00:02:59.800
- Good afternoon,

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:01.880
it's an honor to be here today.

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:03.460
My name is Zen Tang

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:07.023
and I'm a senior in the Stats
and Data Science Department.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:12.780
So I like to start by asking a question.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:15.393
How many of you guys have
heard of the singularity?

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:18.913
I see a lot of hands going up.

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:21.520
And you might think of it as

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:23.620
a point where machine intelligence

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:25.880
and artificial intelligence kind of

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:27.440
rapidly improves

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:31.360
and maybe surpasses human intelligence.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:33.660
You might think of it as a syntheses of

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:36.090
perhaps human and machine

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:39.070
where we work in integrative systems.

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:41.603
You might think of it
as a robot apocalypse

00:03:41.603 --> 00:03:43.000
(laughter)

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:44.510
where the machine just becomes

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:47.270
really good at making paperclips.

00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:50.050
And (laughs) something
that I think scares,

00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:51.140
at least me,

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:52.870
and I'm sure maybe some of you

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:55.147
is that there's this question of,

00:03:55.147 --> 00:03:57.790
"How do we make sure that
the systems we built,

00:03:57.790 --> 00:04:00.940
actually do what we really want them to?"

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:02.450
Now what we say we want them to,

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:04.280
but what we really mean.

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:06.970
How can machines that
are made out of silicon

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:09.260
and programming languages

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:11.490
ever comprehend what it means to feel?

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:12.350
What it means

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:13.750
what we value in life?

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:15.660
How we live and act?

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:18.260
And how we conduct ourselves
with our fellow humans.

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:22.750
I think one problem here
is the issue of trust.

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:24.300
Part of these issues are,

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:27.340
do we have faith that
the machine that we built

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:30.613
will in fact satisfy these specifications?

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:33.660
As computer scientist,

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:36.740
we might like to have
some kind of guarantee,

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:37.573
we might wanna say,

00:04:37.573 --> 00:04:40.010
"Okay, Here are certain criteria

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:42.897
that our systems will satisfy."

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:45.670
We might wanna prove that for example,

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:47.590
these systems can't go havoc.

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:48.550
They can't for example,

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:50.603
turn into a totalitarian regime.

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:54.690
Now,

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:57.080
some of the questions involved here

00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:58.140
might seem pretty new.

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:00.790
For example autonomous car systems

00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:02.940
have been around only
for a couple of time.

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:05.640
But as a matter of fact,

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:07.930
these questions are actually really old.

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:08.960
They've been some of the questions

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:10.570
that we've been thinking
about for hundreds

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:12.750
if not thousands of years.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:15.560
We've actually had super-intelligences

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:18.173
for almost as long as
we've had civilization.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:22.360
Now what do these super-intelligent
systems look like?

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:24.250
I mean I don't see any AI here.

00:05:25.641 --> 00:05:28.120
They look like governments,

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:30.250
systems of law.

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:32.020
Maybe economies,

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:35.390
these organization of people and machines

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:38.090
can do things that none of us ever could.

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:40.330
They integrate terabytes if not exabytes

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:41.760
of information everyday,

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:44.880
shape the flow of
information and resources

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:46.410
across the whole world

00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:49.890
and sometimes do things
that we wouldn't really want

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:51.280
or expect from our systems.

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:52.940
Things like war, famine

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:55.903
and other things.

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:59.040
Now the people who ask
these kinds of questions,

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:00.780
I'm actually sorry for the yellow,

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:01.934
that might be hard to read.

00:06:01.934 --> 00:06:04.640
(chuckles)

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:06.310
So people asks these kind of questions

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:07.970
are generally regarded perhaps

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:09.290
as political theorist,

00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:10.360
economist,

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:11.193
philosophers,

00:06:11.193 --> 00:06:13.023
artists and writers.

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:15.317
These questions are,

00:06:15.317 --> 00:06:18.500
"Do these systems actually
act from my best interest?

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:21.610
Do they act in the best
interest of society?"

00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:25.500
and if we're in charge of
designing these systems

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:26.997
as a society,

00:06:26.997 --> 00:06:27.990
"How do we design them

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:30.360
so that they have the
properties that we want?"

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:32.510
How do we design them so that they ensure

00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:35.520
for example freedom and
equality for everyone?

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:36.790
How do we design these systems

00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:39.080
so that they are safeguarded in place,

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:42.230
so that if these systems ever do fail

00:06:42.230 --> 00:06:45.020
we have some insurance that
they won't fail terribly.

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:47.200
Things that the founding
fathers we're doing

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:49.530
when they founded America

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:51.650
when they wrote down the constitution.

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:52.483
Things that people do

00:06:52.483 --> 00:06:54.470
when they talk about checks and balances.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:59.143
Things like taxation or social goods.

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:03.520
Now as part of this talk

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:04.420
I would like to share with you

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:07.580
a vision for our democracy,

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:09.333
and how this one apply to us today.

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:11.880
A lot of democratic theorist think

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:16.290
that democracy in its
current form is not fixed.

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:18.260
Democracy is in fact malleable

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:20.640
is subject to change

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:21.920
and a lot of different systems

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:23.220
it might not even look like

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:25.923
what we think of democracy
as we think of it today.

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:27.900
For example,

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:29.500
how many of you think that voting is

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:31.423
absolutely essential to democracy?

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:36.160
How many of you think that deliberation

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:38.300
is absolutely essential?

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:40.630
Can we have one without the other?

00:07:40.630 --> 00:07:41.490
Possibly not.

00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:43.340
Is one more important than the other?

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:45.970
Can we have different forms of voting?

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:48.550
And these are questions that
a lot of people have explored

00:07:48.550 --> 00:07:51.403
in recent years but also in years past.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:54.770
And this kind of comes out a time where

00:07:54.770 --> 00:07:58.580
democracy is kind of hit
by seemingly recent forces.

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:00.640
For example the rise of fake news,

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:02.290
political non-participation

00:08:02.290 --> 00:08:04.450
and these criticisms of
democracy are something

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:06.500
that we have to think about

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:07.470
both in terms of

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:09.410
as citizens,

00:08:09.410 --> 00:08:10.870
what they mean to us

00:08:10.870 --> 00:08:13.280
but also in terms of system designers.

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:15.110
How do we design

00:08:15.110 --> 00:08:18.863
new systems so that these
issues can be perhaps mitigated?

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:20.753
I'd like to share with you

00:08:20.753 --> 00:08:24.950
a few ideas that a lot of
really cool people have had.

00:08:24.950 --> 00:08:27.100
For example, one of the professors

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:29.920
in the Political Science
Department here Hélène Landemore

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:32.030
has proposed an idea of open democracy

00:08:32.930 --> 00:08:36.950
which is kind of taking from
the Harbermassian point of view

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:39.800
where you have systems
of deliberate discourse

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:43.140
there are kind of centered around

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:46.073
exchanging ideas between people.

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:48.290
So for example,

00:08:48.290 --> 00:08:52.740
you might elect a randomly
selected body of citizens

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:56.000
and those citizens could
deliberate in the best interest

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:57.010
of all citizens

00:08:57.010 --> 00:08:58.870
and if they were a random sample

00:08:58.870 --> 00:09:00.937
perhaps they would kind
of encapsulate the kind of

00:09:00.937 --> 00:09:04.343
interest that people in general have.

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:07.510
You might also think about for example,

00:09:07.510 --> 00:09:09.170
systems like liquid democracy

00:09:10.170 --> 00:09:13.060
where you would give your vote perhaps,

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:14.670
to someone that you trust,

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:17.440
so instead of everyone voting
for a State Representative,

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:18.670
voting for President.

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:20.310
You just give your vote to someone

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:23.003
and they could vote on your
behalf on certain issues,

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:26.270
you might also think about

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:28.130
ideas about context

00:09:28.130 --> 00:09:33.110
and what kind of forums we
might need for democracy.

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:33.943
For example,

00:09:33.943 --> 00:09:38.760
would Facebook be a good forum
to discuss certain issues?

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:41.740
And here we found that
Facebook has a lot of

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:43.990
interesting things with it.

00:09:44.929 --> 00:09:48.500
And so with technology comes
a certain responsibility

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:50.050
but also a certain opportunity.

00:09:54.530 --> 00:09:56.540
So I'd like to share with you some ideas

00:09:56.540 --> 00:10:01.540
for bringing these kinds
of theories into practice.

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:07.030
And giving us a sense of
where do we go from here?

00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:10.160
And how do humans and technology interact

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:11.583
to form government systems?

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:16.640
So a lot of experiments on democracy

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:18.750
have been smaller scale.

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:21.120
They've revolved around
citizen participation

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:23.980
very specific issues and

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:25.260
a lot of

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:27.480
the process of government
that we have today

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:29.027
are actually really
effective at what they do.

00:10:29.027 --> 00:10:31.610
For example, think of
the City of New Haven

00:10:31.610 --> 00:10:32.700
or the United States

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:35.630
we have the kind of balance
a lot of different issues.

00:10:35.630 --> 00:10:37.280
So one proposal is to test,

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:38.760
new ideas of democracy

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:41.227
by having an online virtual platform.

00:10:41.227 --> 00:10:43.770
And this platform would
actually kind of abstract

00:10:43.770 --> 00:10:47.850
over core fundamental
identities like people and votes

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:49.690
and give you a set of primitives

00:10:49.690 --> 00:10:51.453
on which to build new democracy.

00:10:52.550 --> 00:10:56.460
So one experiment that I've
been kind of working on

00:10:56.460 --> 00:11:00.060
is kind of gathering a set of people to

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:02.390
kind of build the first version of this

00:11:02.390 --> 00:11:04.370
democratic infrastructure.

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:08.810
In which you would have a layer
for the force of foundation

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:11.060
for building new kinds of assemblies.

00:11:11.060 --> 00:11:12.790
A different forms of referenda,

00:11:12.790 --> 00:11:14.370
different forms of voting

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:18.470
and making that available to researchers

00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:20.060
to people in the communities,

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:21.050
to kind of figure out

00:11:21.050 --> 00:11:22.880
where that kind of technology would fit

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:25.330
and how it would work in
our existing government.

00:11:28.930 --> 00:11:31.980
So our idea is to focus on

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:34.680
smaller scale municipal governments

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:37.160
just because its easier
to work with people

00:11:38.120 --> 00:11:40.010
in a local context

00:11:40.010 --> 00:11:42.080
and towns are also kind of big enough

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:42.913
so that

00:11:44.390 --> 00:11:47.263
you could have interesting
democratic experiments.

00:11:48.410 --> 00:11:51.150
And this is kind of the
starting phases of this research

00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:52.750
but we're kind of thinking about

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:56.140
you know kind of expanding
it to more people

00:11:56.140 --> 00:11:59.180
getting more people
involved in thinking about

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:00.013
some of these issues

00:12:00.013 --> 00:12:02.470
like what kind of value should we kind of

00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:05.193
think about as we build out these systems.

00:12:07.730 --> 00:12:09.826
And yeah do we have any questions?

00:12:09.826 --> 00:12:11.970
- That's actually I wanna thank Zen

00:12:11.970 --> 00:12:13.100
for being our first speaker

00:12:13.100 --> 00:12:14.550
and we're actually going to hold questions

00:12:14.550 --> 00:12:17.170
for the poster session in the next room,

00:12:17.170 --> 00:12:19.730
because I know that folks are
all gonna have interesting

00:12:19.730 --> 00:12:20.900
interactions with our speakers

00:12:20.900 --> 00:12:22.766
but thank you so much for going first.

00:12:22.766 --> 00:12:25.016
(applause)

00:12:28.950 --> 00:12:31.010
- While continuing the theme of

00:12:31.010 --> 00:12:32.550
Other Ways of Organization,

00:12:32.550 --> 00:12:34.900
we have a talk next by Frances Fagan

00:12:34.900 --> 00:12:38.530
on Utilitarian Utopias:
Public Libraries as Key Policy

00:12:38.530 --> 00:12:42.160
Partners for Integration and
Skills Training Programs.

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:43.270
Let's see if I can find it here.

00:12:43.270 --> 00:12:44.970
- I don't know how to use this so.

00:12:49.030 --> 00:12:50.270
Do I just,

00:12:50.270 --> 00:12:51.790
okay.

00:12:51.790 --> 00:12:53.580
Thank you all for coming,

00:12:53.580 --> 00:12:54.453
thank you.

00:12:55.300 --> 00:12:57.550
I would especially like to thank the

00:12:57.550 --> 00:13:00.770
Franke Family Digital Humanities Lab Staff

00:13:00.770 --> 00:13:04.340
for their ongoing support
and guidance in our research.

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:06.930
I feel like the field
of Digital Humanities

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:09.280
is particularly important today,

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:10.680
we're experience what has been called

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:12.860
a fourth Industrial Revolution

00:13:12.860 --> 00:13:14.960
which is marked by dramatic transformation

00:13:17.540 --> 00:13:20.700
in both the technologies
that impact our working lives

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:22.180
and the ethnographic changes

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:24.250
that are reshaping our communities.

00:13:24.250 --> 00:13:27.580
And the visualization tools
that Digital Humanities provides

00:13:27.580 --> 00:13:31.060
allow us to asses and share
these changes with others

00:13:31.060 --> 00:13:33.737
as we will see in the conversations today

00:13:33.737 --> 00:13:36.650
and the posters that
will be presented later.

00:13:36.650 --> 00:13:37.900
So I'm Frances Fagan

00:13:37.900 --> 00:13:41.330
I'm a political science
student here at Yale University

00:13:41.330 --> 00:13:42.610
and my interest lie in

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:46.850
Social Integration and Labor Policy,

00:13:46.850 --> 00:13:49.930
particularly how programs
related to social integration

00:13:49.930 --> 00:13:53.200
and skills training are
coordinated and implemented

00:13:53.200 --> 00:13:56.300
at the local and municipal level.

00:13:56.300 --> 00:14:00.540
And it's convenient that
we are in a library today

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:04.150
because I am fascinated
by these institutions.

00:14:04.150 --> 00:14:07.630
So here this image allows us to see

00:14:08.690 --> 00:14:11.050
all of Connecticut's public libraries

00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:13.080
and because of their branch systems,

00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:14.780
public libraries frequently have

00:14:14.780 --> 00:14:16.980
built-in network characteristics

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:19.010
which allow for coordinated programming

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:22.150
and the sharing of best practices.

00:14:22.150 --> 00:14:24.100
So bringing us into New Haven,

00:14:24.100 --> 00:14:26.560
this compact image
taken from the New Haven

00:14:26.560 --> 00:14:28.760
free public library's website

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:31.930
shows us that there are five
public libraries in the city

00:14:31.930 --> 00:14:33.970
and a book mobile.

00:14:33.970 --> 00:14:38.420
It also allows us to look at
user trends for the library

00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:40.993
and their services in 2018.

00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:44.620
And I'm not sure what your perceptions

00:14:44.620 --> 00:14:46.970
of public libraries are

00:14:46.970 --> 00:14:50.410
but they have been drastically changing.

00:14:50.410 --> 00:14:52.290
So they are adapting their services

00:14:53.170 --> 00:14:56.040
in programming to meet
the very demands posed

00:14:56.040 --> 00:14:58.983
by changes in social
demographic and labor needs.

00:14:59.930 --> 00:15:01.360
The books are still popular,

00:15:01.360 --> 00:15:03.403
as we can see in this infographic,

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:09.720
there is a constant and
steady increase in WiFi usage

00:15:09.720 --> 00:15:11.050
and program attendance,

00:15:11.050 --> 00:15:14.320
and these programs range from
story times for new parents

00:15:15.580 --> 00:15:17.360
to coding courses for children

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:19.130
to resume workshops and more.

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:22.440
And these trends and programs
are consistent across

00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:23.970
developed nations as well.

00:15:23.970 --> 00:15:28.840
And I can share from France, the UK

00:15:28.840 --> 00:15:30.963
ask me questions about libraries please.

00:15:32.670 --> 00:15:36.300
And here we see a selection
of digital courses offered

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:38.850
for free on a monthly
basis right down the street

00:15:38.850 --> 00:15:41.070
at Ives Public Library.

00:15:41.070 --> 00:15:43.280
A key service offered by public libraries

00:15:43.280 --> 00:15:45.240
is digital literacy,

00:15:45.240 --> 00:15:47.550
defined by the American
Library Association

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:48.607
as a skill that quote,

00:15:48.607 --> 00:15:51.440
"Enables the individual to
recognize when information

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:53.660
is needed and gives them the ability to

00:15:53.660 --> 00:15:57.347
locate, evaluate and use that
information more effectively."

00:15:58.630 --> 00:16:00.640
It's not enough to simply have access

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:02.633
to a smartphone or a computer.

00:16:07.670 --> 00:16:10.280
In order to stay in a
truly democratic society

00:16:10.280 --> 00:16:12.370
all members must be able to navigate

00:16:12.370 --> 00:16:15.400
increasingly complex technologies

00:16:15.400 --> 00:16:17.410
much as we are doing in this symposium

00:16:17.410 --> 00:16:19.483
on Digital Humanities today right?

00:16:20.810 --> 00:16:22.900
Examples of Digital Humanity

00:16:22.900 --> 00:16:26.770
Digital Literacy here range
from free classes on how to

00:16:26.770 --> 00:16:29.120
asses and analyze everyday information,

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:31.800
like the infographics in these PowerPoints

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:35.270
to establishing professional
networks through LinkedIn.

00:16:35.270 --> 00:16:38.750
This variety of free
training is impressive

00:16:38.750 --> 00:16:40.270
yet if we look at the relationship

00:16:40.270 --> 00:16:43.410
between government agencies
and public libraries networks

00:16:43.410 --> 00:16:45.313
we see a lack of coordination.

00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:49.940
This is Connecticut's 2-1-1,

00:16:49.940 --> 00:16:51.980
which is the state site

00:16:51.980 --> 00:16:54.200
that is intended to serve
as a one stop portal

00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:58.100
linking users to essential
services throughout the state.

00:16:58.100 --> 00:17:00.620
Yet when I enter job
training formats in New Haven

00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:02.427
only one option came up

00:17:02.427 --> 00:17:03.500
and as we can see

00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:06.123
it's a Pre-employment
Background Check site.

00:17:08.170 --> 00:17:10.020
Related searches linked to agencies

00:17:10.020 --> 00:17:11.850
which often had barriers to entry

00:17:11.850 --> 00:17:15.410
including age, income and veteran status.

00:17:15.410 --> 00:17:17.910
The lack of coordination
between government agencies

00:17:17.910 --> 00:17:20.230
working with integration and labor policy

00:17:20.230 --> 00:17:22.350
in relevant public library programming

00:17:22.350 --> 00:17:25.000
is not unique to the United States.

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:27.600
Although both the European
Commission and OECD

00:17:27.600 --> 00:17:29.610
stress the urgent need to provide

00:17:29.610 --> 00:17:31.600
basic educational programming

00:17:31.600 --> 00:17:35.500
and digital literacy training
throughout the United States

00:17:35.500 --> 00:17:39.010
their policy prescriptions rarely if ever

00:17:39.010 --> 00:17:43.220
mention public library
networks as potential partners.

00:17:43.220 --> 00:17:46.240
And if there are any library
librarians in the room

00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:48.923
you know how extensive these networks are.

00:17:51.180 --> 00:17:52.540
All right so in addition to these

00:17:52.540 --> 00:17:55.370
more targeted digital literacy programming

00:17:55.370 --> 00:17:56.840
there are countless opportunities

00:17:56.840 --> 00:17:59.640
to engage in diverse
social and cultural events

00:17:59.640 --> 00:18:01.460
in public libraries.

00:18:01.460 --> 00:18:04.530
Such encounters can strengthen
community relationships

00:18:04.530 --> 00:18:08.240
in what can be politically and
economically fraught times.

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:11.590
Here we see examples of
some of these programs

00:18:11.590 --> 00:18:13.550
that libraries in New Haven

00:18:13.550 --> 00:18:15.750
as well as an image of the newly remodeled

00:18:15.750 --> 00:18:20.290
Ives Squared Space were
among other cool events.

00:18:20.290 --> 00:18:22.650
Entrepreneurs can get
advice on their start-ups

00:18:22.650 --> 00:18:25.810
from external partners
and residential fellas.

00:18:25.810 --> 00:18:27.070
Earlier today in fact

00:18:27.070 --> 00:18:30.410
there was a meetup of game developers

00:18:31.740 --> 00:18:34.620
where participants from
all ages and skill levels

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:37.553
came together to share
advice and get support.

00:18:38.950 --> 00:18:40.310
I would definitely recommend

00:18:40.310 --> 00:18:42.270
if you hadn't been to Ives Square

00:18:42.270 --> 00:18:44.560
or have been in to the
New Haven Public Library

00:18:44.560 --> 00:18:46.760
since they renovated
to go and check it out.

00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:50.810
So I conclude by pulling back

00:18:50.810 --> 00:18:53.280
to this overview of
Public Library Networks

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:54.537
on the state level

00:18:54.537 --> 00:18:56.220
and I ask that we reconsider

00:18:56.220 --> 00:18:59.330
not only the density of these institutions

00:18:59.330 --> 00:19:02.910
but also the reach of their
extended networks of patrons,

00:19:02.910 --> 00:19:05.713
government agencies and
external partnerships.

00:19:06.620 --> 00:19:08.740
How might these connections better serve

00:19:08.740 --> 00:19:10.963
the needs of contemporary societies?

00:19:12.050 --> 00:19:14.330
And perhaps more importantly

00:19:14.330 --> 00:19:17.683
what is your public
library up to right now?

00:19:19.290 --> 00:19:20.650
Thanks you so much

00:19:20.650 --> 00:19:22.860
I look forward to learning about

00:19:22.860 --> 00:19:24.560
Digital Humanities research with you

00:19:24.560 --> 00:19:27.140
and to answering any
questions that you might have.

00:19:27.140 --> 00:19:28.082
Thank you

00:19:28.082 --> 00:19:30.332
(applause)

00:19:36.382 --> 00:19:39.090
- Thanks so much to Frances for that

00:19:39.090 --> 00:19:42.530
and I'd like to call up for
our next speaker Keniel Yao

00:19:42.530 --> 00:19:43.430
who's gonna speak about

00:19:43.430 --> 00:19:46.673
Virtual Politics: A Case Study of Reddit.

00:19:51.610 --> 00:19:52.530
Great thank you so much

00:19:52.530 --> 00:19:54.420
it's such a pleasure to
be with you guys today

00:19:54.420 --> 00:19:56.120
and I'm really excited about the work

00:19:56.120 --> 00:19:58.850
and that Beyond Boundaries does.

00:19:58.850 --> 00:20:02.240
Specifically in my own interest
as a statistics student

00:20:02.240 --> 00:20:03.260
whose first love

00:20:03.260 --> 00:20:05.600
intellectual love really was anthropology.

00:20:05.600 --> 00:20:08.900
I think there are increasing
worlds of social research which

00:20:08.900 --> 00:20:10.890
necessitate and demand

00:20:10.890 --> 00:20:12.510
a data science approach

00:20:12.510 --> 00:20:14.900
and today's research
that I'll be presenting

00:20:14.900 --> 00:20:17.943
from my senior thesis
lent itself today as well.

00:20:19.090 --> 00:20:20.810
The idea I see data science

00:20:20.810 --> 00:20:23.030
and its section of anthropology is this,

00:20:23.030 --> 00:20:27.103
specifically that when we're
looking at online lives,

00:20:28.550 --> 00:20:30.310
how do we listen to data?

00:20:30.310 --> 00:20:32.920
Well it's specifically with statistics

00:20:32.920 --> 00:20:35.140
and it suits political life online

00:20:35.140 --> 00:20:37.290
especially on word platforms like Reddit

00:20:37.290 --> 00:20:39.880
specifically because users are anonymous

00:20:39.880 --> 00:20:41.640
and they age geographical.

00:20:41.640 --> 00:20:43.623
And so those are approaches and words that

00:20:43.623 --> 00:20:47.160
aren't often associated with
ethnographic research where

00:20:47.160 --> 00:20:50.100
you're necessarily part of
the community in a space

00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:51.840
with people who have names.

00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:55.180
And so statistics seem
to be a much more natural

00:20:55.180 --> 00:20:56.930
lends itself more naturally to this

00:20:58.720 --> 00:21:00.660
The research I'll be presenting on today,

00:21:00.660 --> 00:21:02.190
is really focusing on this question,

00:21:02.190 --> 00:21:03.320
what does a like mean?

00:21:03.320 --> 00:21:05.120
What is a good political comment?

00:21:05.120 --> 00:21:08.770
And so this research focuses
on the politics subreddit,

00:21:08.770 --> 00:21:10.780
which is a sub community within Reddit

00:21:10.780 --> 00:21:12.885
focused on U.S. politics

00:21:12.885 --> 00:21:16.670
analyze this data from 2008 to 2018

00:21:16.670 --> 00:21:18.530
just for a sense of scale on this data

00:21:18.530 --> 00:21:23.379
there's about a terabyte worth
across which is all stored

00:21:23.379 --> 00:21:25.530
every single post and comment on Reddit

00:21:25.530 --> 00:21:26.960
is stored online on Google Cloud

00:21:26.960 --> 00:21:28.543
and is extremely accessible.

00:21:31.120 --> 00:21:32.430
Cool

00:21:32.430 --> 00:21:34.510
Let's see so it's interesting
because I feel like

00:21:34.510 --> 00:21:37.460
a lot of social research
focuses on Facebook and Twitter

00:21:37.460 --> 00:21:41.300
and any of these don't seem to
lend themselves as easily to

00:21:41.300 --> 00:21:42.530
statistical research because

00:21:42.530 --> 00:21:44.100
there's a lot of privacy concerns.

00:21:44.100 --> 00:21:47.960
Whereas Reddit is
inherently a public website.

00:21:47.960 --> 00:21:50.410
It's known as the front
page of the internet

00:21:50.410 --> 00:21:51.980
and surprisingly for a lot of people,

00:21:51.980 --> 00:21:52.820
I don't think they know that

00:21:52.820 --> 00:21:55.350
this was actually the third
most clicked upon website

00:21:55.350 --> 00:21:56.640
in 2018.

00:21:56.640 --> 00:21:58.560
Just after Google and after Facebook,

00:21:58.560 --> 00:21:59.780
after Google and after YouTube

00:21:59.780 --> 00:22:02.710
so before Twitter and Facebook.

00:22:02.710 --> 00:22:04.460
Additionally in terms of engagement

00:22:05.600 --> 00:22:07.710
based on its population of users

00:22:07.710 --> 00:22:09.880
there are more clicks on

00:22:10.970 --> 00:22:14.630
more clicks on Reddit per
user than any other site.

00:22:14.630 --> 00:22:16.380
So in terms of the richness of engagement

00:22:16.380 --> 00:22:17.270
and the richness of data

00:22:17.270 --> 00:22:20.220
Reddit really seems to
be a promising tool.

00:22:20.220 --> 00:22:21.660
But there's something special about

00:22:21.660 --> 00:22:24.090
the politics sub community on Reddit

00:22:24.090 --> 00:22:25.860
specifically that with Reddit

00:22:26.900 --> 00:22:29.670
each subreddit has its own set of rules.

00:22:29.670 --> 00:22:30.503
And so here,

00:22:30.503 --> 00:22:32.520
the unique thing about politics is that

00:22:32.520 --> 00:22:35.370
users are only allowed
to post news articles.

00:22:35.370 --> 00:22:37.170
Specifically when they post

00:22:37.170 --> 00:22:40.340
the headline must be identical
to the news article website

00:22:40.340 --> 00:22:43.610
and content must be a
link to the news article.

00:22:43.610 --> 00:22:46.040
Which means that users
can't impose anything else

00:22:46.040 --> 00:22:48.900
and interpretation and
political commentary

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:50.463
can only occur in the comments.

00:22:53.040 --> 00:22:55.160
It's really interesting to
look at the distribution

00:22:55.160 --> 00:22:57.410
of some of the articles here.

00:22:57.410 --> 00:22:59.590
I apologize for the lack
of labeling on the y-axis.

00:22:59.590 --> 00:23:01.390
But what that means is

00:23:01.390 --> 00:23:04.340
I use the topic model to assess
all of the news headlines

00:23:04.340 --> 00:23:05.610
that had been posted on Reddit

00:23:05.610 --> 00:23:08.220
from 2008 to 2018

00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:10.090
and group them using topic modeling

00:23:10.090 --> 00:23:12.940
and then using a walk
trap clustering algorithm

00:23:12.940 --> 00:23:14.330
into six main themes.

00:23:14.330 --> 00:23:16.513
So the first one reflects foreign policy.

00:23:17.350 --> 00:23:18.560
It's really interesting that like

00:23:18.560 --> 00:23:21.910
It's kind of stagnant until after 2016

00:23:21.910 --> 00:23:24.650
what actually happens is that Trump

00:23:24.650 --> 00:23:27.150
gets associated with a
lot of foreign policy

00:23:27.150 --> 00:23:28.430
because of the Nuland Investigation

00:23:28.430 --> 00:23:29.620
and because of Russia.

00:23:29.620 --> 00:23:30.550
And so those topics

00:23:30.550 --> 00:23:32.763
actually changed their meaning after 2016.

00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:38.000
The graph number two
indicates popular issues

00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:40.740
which remains relatively
static across all time,

00:23:40.740 --> 00:23:43.883
the increase is just an increase
in population of Reddit.

00:23:45.420 --> 00:23:47.390
Let's see number three is election issues

00:23:47.390 --> 00:23:50.710
which of course rise up in 2012 and 2016

00:23:50.710 --> 00:23:53.090
but particularly after 2016.

00:23:53.090 --> 00:23:54.910
The bump in 2016 with number four

00:23:54.910 --> 00:23:57.163
actually indicates campaign corruption.

00:23:58.540 --> 00:24:00.410
Which shows it bubbles up in 2016

00:24:00.410 --> 00:24:01.840
and dies down again.

00:24:01.840 --> 00:24:03.830
And number five talked
about economic policy

00:24:03.830 --> 00:24:05.700
which is pretty stagnant overall.

00:24:05.700 --> 00:24:07.340
And so there's really
cool topic modeling things

00:24:07.340 --> 00:24:09.960
that you can do with the whole sub Reddit

00:24:09.960 --> 00:24:12.510
and a lot of really interesting
topics come out as well.

00:24:12.510 --> 00:24:15.050
So you see the Keystone
pipeline becoming its own topic,

00:24:15.050 --> 00:24:17.010
you see "Fire and Fury"
becoming its own topic,

00:24:17.010 --> 00:24:19.390
Nuland investigation becoming
its own topic as well.

00:24:19.390 --> 00:24:21.140
It's a really really clean dataset.

00:24:22.490 --> 00:24:24.190
So the key question coming across,

00:24:25.230 --> 00:24:27.560
here are particularly
interesting rules that

00:24:27.560 --> 00:24:28.650
the moderators have placed

00:24:28.650 --> 00:24:29.710
but they can't enforce

00:24:29.710 --> 00:24:33.140
is that they want people users on politics

00:24:33.140 --> 00:24:36.180
to vote basically like or dislike comments

00:24:36.180 --> 00:24:37.500
based on the quality

00:24:37.500 --> 00:24:39.400
and not on the opinion of the comment.

00:24:40.350 --> 00:24:42.920
You can't enforce it
because likes are anonymous

00:24:42.920 --> 00:24:44.820
and because there's ton of them right.

00:24:45.889 --> 00:24:47.560
And so it's interesting to see

00:24:47.560 --> 00:24:49.320
what actually happens.

00:24:49.320 --> 00:24:51.883
Which is the focus of this talk.

00:24:53.200 --> 00:24:55.640
This is an example of a post,

00:24:55.640 --> 00:24:57.170
one of the most popular posts of all time

00:24:57.170 --> 00:25:00.220
which is Donald Trump
becoming president from AP

00:25:00.220 --> 00:25:01.757
and the first comment says,

00:25:01.757 --> 00:25:05.793
"4 Dem Faithless electors from
WA: 3 Powell 1 Spotted Eagle"

00:25:06.990 --> 00:25:09.030
what's interesting about
Reddit again is that

00:25:09.030 --> 00:25:11.650
there is no personalization of feeds.

00:25:11.650 --> 00:25:13.830
You can subscribe to different communities

00:25:13.830 --> 00:25:15.330
but within a community

00:25:15.330 --> 00:25:17.970
everyone sees exactly the
same thing in the same order.

00:25:17.970 --> 00:25:20.230
Both post wise and comment wise

00:25:20.230 --> 00:25:21.690
and these are related

00:25:21.690 --> 00:25:23.520
this order is determined by the popularity

00:25:23.520 --> 00:25:25.849
which is likes and the downvotes

00:25:25.849 --> 00:25:27.193
of each comment.

00:25:28.290 --> 00:25:29.773
And so here we can,

00:25:30.960 --> 00:25:32.710
if you're interested you can transfer

00:25:32.710 --> 00:25:35.040
every single post into
a tree-like structure

00:25:35.040 --> 00:25:37.050
where because you can
have comments comments

00:25:37.050 --> 00:25:37.980
to comments et cetera

00:25:37.980 --> 00:25:40.237
you can build out what
long comment chains are

00:25:40.237 --> 00:25:42.103
and how users are inter-related.

00:25:43.910 --> 00:25:45.730
But here's the experiment,

00:25:45.730 --> 00:25:47.110
if we're thinking about

00:25:48.500 --> 00:25:50.130
what makes a comment successful

00:25:50.130 --> 00:25:52.600
or what makes people
like a certain comment.

00:25:52.600 --> 00:25:54.603
I framed it in terms of this hypothesis,

00:25:55.540 --> 00:25:58.070
running a classifier to see where a model

00:25:58.070 --> 00:26:00.110
using just generic

00:26:00.110 --> 00:26:01.710
generic variable so for example,

00:26:01.710 --> 00:26:02.990
how popular the post was,

00:26:02.990 --> 00:26:04.510
where it came from news article,

00:26:04.510 --> 00:26:06.100
and when it was posted.

00:26:06.100 --> 00:26:08.240
I'm comparing that with
specific language features,

00:26:08.240 --> 00:26:09.690
which one is more predictive.

00:26:11.240 --> 00:26:12.210
The idea is that

00:26:12.210 --> 00:26:14.110
if language features can
actually help prediction

00:26:14.110 --> 00:26:16.590
we can also detect which
language specifically

00:26:16.590 --> 00:26:18.010
which language feature actually

00:26:18.010 --> 00:26:21.010
lead to a comment being
associated with a higher

00:26:21.010 --> 00:26:22.793
higher score or a lower score.

00:26:24.520 --> 00:26:26.740
And so I run this with logistic regression

00:26:26.740 --> 00:26:27.980
basically what that happens

00:26:27.980 --> 00:26:29.700
is its classifying comments

00:26:29.700 --> 00:26:34.340
into either popular comments
or not popular comments.

00:26:34.340 --> 00:26:35.930
Where popular comments is defined

00:26:35.930 --> 00:26:37.980
as ones who make the top three

00:26:37.980 --> 00:26:40.980
the top three most liked comments
off all time on that post.

00:26:43.410 --> 00:26:44.320
If you're interested,

00:26:44.320 --> 00:26:46.650
the language features
comes from a program called

00:26:46.650 --> 00:26:49.410
LIWC or Linguistic Inquiry and Word Count

00:26:49.410 --> 00:26:52.530
which is a state of the art psychometric

00:26:52.530 --> 00:26:54.140
language analysis tool.

00:26:54.140 --> 00:26:55.680
It labels

00:26:55.680 --> 00:26:56.980
comment or any sort of text

00:26:56.980 --> 00:26:58.720
with positive and negative emotions

00:26:58.720 --> 00:27:00.860
but also things like

00:27:00.860 --> 00:27:02.170
how analytical it is

00:27:02.170 --> 00:27:03.690
how much power is used

00:27:03.690 --> 00:27:05.480
authenticity, emotional tone.

00:27:05.480 --> 00:27:06.590
So these are a lot of interesting

00:27:06.590 --> 00:27:07.840
and rich in variable coming

00:27:07.840 --> 00:27:09.850
from the psychological
literature that we can

00:27:09.850 --> 00:27:11.363
easily label our text with.

00:27:14.520 --> 00:27:17.470
I analyze my data on
2012, 2016, and 2018 data

00:27:17.470 --> 00:27:19.673
but just here are some
of the brief results.

00:27:20.910 --> 00:27:23.330
You'd expect 50% to be like

00:27:23.330 --> 00:27:24.350
just a random prediction

00:27:24.350 --> 00:27:26.460
of whether a comment is successful enough

00:27:26.460 --> 00:27:29.350
and we achieve about a 70% accuracy

00:27:29.350 --> 00:27:30.420
with both the baseline

00:27:30.420 --> 00:27:32.450
and with the language feature model.

00:27:32.450 --> 00:27:34.420
There's no significant
difference between the baseline

00:27:34.420 --> 00:27:35.970
and the language feature model.

00:27:36.830 --> 00:27:38.180
Precision and recall what that means,

00:27:38.180 --> 00:27:39.130
precision is

00:27:39.130 --> 00:27:41.020
if I label a comment as
being in the top three

00:27:41.020 --> 00:27:43.150
how many of them are
truly in the top three.

00:27:43.150 --> 00:27:46.130
And we get about 2/3 accuracy on that

00:27:46.130 --> 00:27:48.840
and recall is of all of
the top three comments

00:27:48.840 --> 00:27:50.950
how many of do I actually
pick up as correct.

00:27:50.950 --> 00:27:53.800
And I get about an 80% accuracy on that.

00:27:53.800 --> 00:27:55.470
And so it performs much better than chance

00:27:55.470 --> 00:27:57.850
but there's a few more
parameters that we can tweak

00:27:57.850 --> 00:28:01.300
that I'll do later on
that are interesting.

00:28:01.300 --> 00:28:03.420
In terms of predictive models

00:28:03.420 --> 00:28:04.750
here are some of the language features

00:28:04.750 --> 00:28:07.420
that the model picks up on.

00:28:07.420 --> 00:28:10.800
And so we see that comments
which are analytical

00:28:10.800 --> 00:28:13.670
and emotional both tend to lend themselves

00:28:13.670 --> 00:28:15.650
towards high popularity.

00:28:15.650 --> 00:28:17.480
The contrast there is interesting

00:28:17.480 --> 00:28:19.030
but then we can also look at

00:28:19.030 --> 00:28:20.430
more sophisticated words

00:28:20.430 --> 00:28:22.780
words with longer words

00:28:22.780 --> 00:28:23.613
words in dictionaries

00:28:23.613 --> 00:28:26.560
and things using numbers
are also more predictive

00:28:27.890 --> 00:28:29.270
overcoming being successful.

00:28:29.270 --> 00:28:32.170
Interestingly using
more generic adjectives

00:28:32.170 --> 00:28:34.120
and using informal speech

00:28:34.120 --> 00:28:36.693
associates itself with
not being successful.

00:28:38.510 --> 00:28:41.303
Just for those of you who are
interested in Topic Modeling,

00:28:42.183 --> 00:28:44.090
this is the next kind
of step in the process

00:28:44.090 --> 00:28:46.580
which is if we associated each post

00:28:46.580 --> 00:28:49.070
and article with a theme or a topic

00:28:49.070 --> 00:28:49.903
then after that

00:28:49.903 --> 00:28:51.760
we can really go and see
how language features

00:28:51.760 --> 00:28:53.540
vary with each topic.

00:28:53.540 --> 00:28:55.070
So for example our

00:28:55.070 --> 00:28:58.640
post which have to do with economics more

00:28:58.640 --> 00:29:00.750
more favorable if
they're more quantitative

00:29:00.750 --> 00:29:03.500
whereas if it's more
about political scandals

00:29:03.500 --> 00:29:05.470
do they lend themselves
towards more emotion.

00:29:05.470 --> 00:29:06.330
And so that's possible

00:29:06.330 --> 00:29:09.340
with this next model that
I'm gonna explore later on.

00:29:09.340 --> 00:29:11.767
But yeah thank you for
listening good talk.

00:29:11.767 --> 00:29:14.017
(applause)

00:29:17.730 --> 00:29:19.710
- Thanks so much to Keniel for that,

00:29:19.710 --> 00:29:21.980
and I would like to invite Lis

00:29:21.980 --> 00:29:23.453
I'm sorry Tyler Lutz,

00:29:24.730 --> 00:29:27.510
who's going to be speaking to us on

00:29:28.480 --> 00:29:33.123
Science in Fiction: Modern
Physics as Mirror and Muse.

00:29:40.540 --> 00:29:41.373
- Thank you,

00:29:43.400 --> 00:29:45.080
in conjectures on World Literature

00:29:45.080 --> 00:29:47.450
Franco Moretti articulates
a conception of literature

00:29:47.450 --> 00:29:48.560
as a composite product

00:29:48.560 --> 00:29:51.200
of endogenous phylogenetic
literary developments

00:29:51.200 --> 00:29:53.330
of a given language or tradition

00:29:53.330 --> 00:29:55.430
and waves of exogenous foreign forms

00:29:55.430 --> 00:29:57.650
that continually buff
it and modulate them.

00:29:57.650 --> 00:30:00.040
To read world literature
in Moretti's account

00:30:00.040 --> 00:30:02.350
is to read with a sufficient
distance from the text

00:30:02.350 --> 00:30:04.810
that these global waves
developments like the novel

00:30:04.810 --> 00:30:07.223
or stream of consciousness
come into focus.

00:30:08.420 --> 00:30:09.380
Preempting the criticism

00:30:09.380 --> 00:30:11.600
that any such world
literary critical project

00:30:11.600 --> 00:30:14.260
might undermine the coherence
of national literatures,

00:30:14.260 --> 00:30:15.677
Moretti owns that quote,

00:30:15.677 --> 00:30:17.270
"There is no other justification

00:30:17.270 --> 00:30:19.150
for the study of World
Literature but this,

00:30:19.150 --> 00:30:20.670
to be a thorn on the side.

00:30:20.670 --> 00:30:22.310
A permanent intellectual challenged

00:30:22.310 --> 00:30:23.587
to national literatures."

00:30:24.620 --> 00:30:25.460
My project takes,

00:30:25.460 --> 00:30:27.840
as its original motive the question,

00:30:27.840 --> 00:30:30.570
what is world literature's
thorn in the side?

00:30:30.570 --> 00:30:31.570
Or to put it in other words,

00:30:31.570 --> 00:30:34.450
why stop at planetary boundaries?

00:30:34.450 --> 00:30:36.880
The dizzying expanses
of intergalactic space,

00:30:36.880 --> 00:30:40.120
the humming nonsense propounded
at inscrutably small scales

00:30:40.120 --> 00:30:41.450
and the coordinated chaos

00:30:41.450 --> 00:30:43.210
of global climactic formations

00:30:43.210 --> 00:30:44.210
are all as much sites

00:30:44.210 --> 00:30:45.510
of cultural production

00:30:45.510 --> 00:30:46.930
as any national literature.

00:30:46.930 --> 00:30:48.740
All domains of humans experience

00:30:48.740 --> 00:30:50.570
that we each partake in.

00:30:50.570 --> 00:30:52.920
Where do they fit in Moretti's tidy scheme

00:30:52.920 --> 00:30:55.273
of interconnected
ethnolinguistic polities.

00:30:57.550 --> 00:30:59.860
Quite long in Richard
Jean So's re-stylization

00:30:59.860 --> 00:31:03.170
of Moretti's global
waves as turbulent flow

00:31:03.170 --> 00:31:04.840
takes a crucial step towards highlighting

00:31:04.840 --> 00:31:06.670
that the very figure
under girding Moretti's

00:31:06.670 --> 00:31:10.133
world literature is itself
is a borrowed foreign form.

00:31:11.470 --> 00:31:13.630
These are our meso scale
eddies in the ocean

00:31:13.630 --> 00:31:15.700
compiled by NASA.

00:31:15.700 --> 00:31:18.170
It's only through the
combined cultural production

00:31:18.170 --> 00:31:19.120
of interwoven networks

00:31:19.120 --> 00:31:21.860
not only of economic historians
that Moretti highlights

00:31:21.860 --> 00:31:24.380
but also cartographers,
archeologists, astronauts

00:31:24.380 --> 00:31:26.660
and planetary scientists of all stripes

00:31:26.660 --> 00:31:28.180
that we can even begin to visualize

00:31:28.180 --> 00:31:29.600
the patterns of global circulation

00:31:29.600 --> 00:31:32.590
that are preconditioned
for world literature

00:31:32.590 --> 00:31:34.440
and not only our world
and national literatures

00:31:34.440 --> 00:31:36.210
but two nodes and a vast assemblage

00:31:36.210 --> 00:31:38.120
of interlinked intellectual projects

00:31:38.120 --> 00:31:39.880
that also becomes difficult to describe

00:31:39.880 --> 00:31:41.450
any kind of hierarchy to them.

00:31:41.450 --> 00:31:42.283
The closer we look

00:31:42.283 --> 00:31:44.410
even at ostensibly hyper local forms,

00:31:44.410 --> 00:31:47.039
let's zoom in on one of these waves,

00:31:47.039 --> 00:31:49.920
the more we realize
that the local materials

00:31:49.920 --> 00:31:53.310
that Moretti's imagining are themselves

00:31:53.310 --> 00:31:55.373
subject to cycles of global circulation.

00:31:56.310 --> 00:31:58.650
This is an artwork by Chris Jordan

00:31:58.650 --> 00:32:00.850
made up of 2.4 million pieces of plastic

00:32:00.850 --> 00:32:02.160
that's equivalent to the amount of plastic

00:32:02.160 --> 00:32:04.410
that we put in the ocean every hour.

00:32:04.410 --> 00:32:09.247
You can argue that this is a
bit of a contrived example.

00:32:09.247 --> 00:32:11.190
But let me remind you that
the very English language

00:32:11.190 --> 00:32:12.200
that I'm using right now

00:32:12.200 --> 00:32:14.940
is nothing more than a accreted flotsam

00:32:14.940 --> 00:32:16.773
of many different languages.

00:32:18.820 --> 00:32:20.660
So I wanna take this very
seriously as a model of

00:32:20.660 --> 00:32:21.700
for literature itself

00:32:21.700 --> 00:32:22.960
and if you'll squint at it

00:32:22.960 --> 00:32:23.860
just a little bit,

00:32:25.580 --> 00:32:26.560
we can actually see something

00:32:26.560 --> 00:32:27.920
that looks a lot like literature.

00:32:27.920 --> 00:32:29.190
This is

00:32:29.190 --> 00:32:31.310
to borrow from Keniel's Topic Modeling,

00:32:31.310 --> 00:32:33.950
this is a topic model of
a corpus of fictional text

00:32:33.950 --> 00:32:35.760
taken from haughty trust,

00:32:35.760 --> 00:32:36.593
this represents

00:32:36.593 --> 00:32:39.340
1920 to 1922.

00:32:39.340 --> 00:32:40.890
What we're seeing here every bubble

00:32:40.890 --> 00:32:42.630
represents a cluster of words

00:32:44.868 --> 00:32:46.210
there are groups here

00:32:46.210 --> 00:32:47.240
according to their likely

00:32:47.240 --> 00:32:49.960
the likelihood of their
co-occurrence in different texts

00:32:49.960 --> 00:32:50.900
within the corpus.

00:32:50.900 --> 00:32:53.560
And we can see some kind of
recognizable features here.

00:32:53.560 --> 00:32:55.810
Here one of these topics
looks like a kind of

00:32:56.660 --> 00:32:59.660
courtly fiction upon king, thou, shall

00:33:01.010 --> 00:33:02.440
this might somethin be more like a

00:33:02.440 --> 00:33:04.370
western, camps trail

00:33:04.370 --> 00:33:05.203
there's some rivers,

00:33:05.203 --> 00:33:06.430
some Indians

00:33:06.430 --> 00:33:08.593
and here's a gender domestics based.

00:33:10.102 --> 00:33:12.350
It's not entirely
obvious what these topics

00:33:12.350 --> 00:33:13.330
are supposed to represent though

00:33:13.330 --> 00:33:15.170
can we call them genres?

00:33:15.170 --> 00:33:17.960
Are they somehow micro discourses?

00:33:17.960 --> 00:33:19.720
Little subsets of the language?

00:33:19.720 --> 00:33:20.890
What I wanna argue is that

00:33:20.890 --> 00:33:22.290
what this topic model is showing us

00:33:22.290 --> 00:33:24.220
actually is literature resolved

00:33:24.220 --> 00:33:25.053
very literally

00:33:25.053 --> 00:33:27.440
into its constitutive hermeneutic circles,

00:33:27.440 --> 00:33:29.410
domains in which form and materials

00:33:29.410 --> 00:33:30.893
are mutually co-determining.

00:33:32.160 --> 00:33:32.993
In other words,

00:33:32.993 --> 00:33:34.860
the closer we look at world literature

00:33:34.860 --> 00:33:35.860
the closer we find

00:33:35.860 --> 00:33:37.570
that we end up right
back where we started.

00:33:37.570 --> 00:33:39.183
That these turbulent eddies,

00:33:40.190 --> 00:33:41.550
I'm thinking of World
Literature as a kind of

00:33:41.550 --> 00:33:42.883
fractal pattern.

00:33:44.310 --> 00:33:46.700
There's a subset of these
eddies that I might actually

00:33:46.700 --> 00:33:49.670
somewhat qualified to
talk about as a physicist.

00:33:49.670 --> 00:33:51.723
Words like this entropy.

00:33:52.832 --> 00:33:54.310
As a word,

00:33:54.310 --> 00:33:57.360
entropy was invented in
1980 by Clausius in Germany,

00:33:57.360 --> 00:33:58.860
I'm playing with two
different spellings here

00:33:58.860 --> 00:34:01.180
to account for the
difference in the languages.

00:34:01.180 --> 00:34:03.750
It gets reinvented in the 1950 in a

00:34:03.750 --> 00:34:06.070
by Shannon in a information
theoretic context.

00:34:06.070 --> 00:34:07.310
And ever since then its kind of been

00:34:07.310 --> 00:34:10.393
percolating in to these
different national literatures.

00:34:11.970 --> 00:34:13.190
But true to my argument I think

00:34:13.190 --> 00:34:15.960
I wanna think of entropy
as more than just a word.

00:34:15.960 --> 00:34:17.940
Through the magic of the
Second Law of Thermodynamics

00:34:17.940 --> 00:34:19.240
it contains within it

00:34:19.240 --> 00:34:20.890
an entire narrative in miniature,

00:34:20.890 --> 00:34:21.723
it goes like this,

00:34:21.723 --> 00:34:23.890
"Stuff breaks".

00:34:23.890 --> 00:34:25.450
We can even think of
entropy as designating

00:34:25.450 --> 00:34:26.843
a kind of micro-genre.

00:34:28.310 --> 00:34:29.780
What I hope to do with my work

00:34:29.780 --> 00:34:31.650
is to contribute a small
amount to the project

00:34:31.650 --> 00:34:34.160
the we might as well
call Cosmic Literature,

00:34:34.160 --> 00:34:35.440
by investigating how physics

00:34:35.440 --> 00:34:37.650
as a collection of words and stories

00:34:37.650 --> 00:34:40.670
that are continually folding
back into each other.

00:34:40.670 --> 00:34:43.110
How that physics both
informs and is shaped

00:34:43.110 --> 00:34:44.270
by the material constituents

00:34:44.270 --> 00:34:46.263
and formal determinance of literature.

00:34:48.050 --> 00:34:51.210
One place to start is to disaggregate,

00:34:51.210 --> 00:34:52.810
I'm gonna take the English literatures

00:34:52.810 --> 00:34:53.870
in the bottom two rows here

00:34:53.870 --> 00:34:56.333
and just aggregate them by genre.

00:34:57.170 --> 00:35:00.743
Here's a time series of how
entropy shows up in my corpus.

00:35:02.200 --> 00:35:04.270
This is not something
I would have expected.

00:35:04.270 --> 00:35:07.130
Apparently poets are a lot
more interested in entropy

00:35:07.130 --> 00:35:09.653
than our dramatists and fiction writers.

00:35:11.370 --> 00:35:12.300
If we poke around a bit more

00:35:12.300 --> 00:35:14.210
we can find other words
that show a similar shape.

00:35:14.210 --> 00:35:16.600
Here's centrifugal.

00:35:16.600 --> 00:35:18.360
Centripetal also has a similar shape.

00:35:18.360 --> 00:35:20.500
So does disorder,

00:35:20.500 --> 00:35:21.720
and we can go on and on like this.

00:35:21.720 --> 00:35:22.980
Chaos

00:35:22.980 --> 00:35:24.270
seems to be something that

00:35:24.270 --> 00:35:26.230
poetry is very interested in.

00:35:26.230 --> 00:35:28.340
I wanna argue that's a mistake though,

00:35:28.340 --> 00:35:30.270
I don't either want to say that poetry

00:35:30.270 --> 00:35:31.820
is somehow an inherently chaotic

00:35:31.820 --> 00:35:33.520
disordered dizzying medium

00:35:33.520 --> 00:35:35.970
nor do I wanna say that physics
has somehow been pushing it

00:35:35.970 --> 00:35:37.610
in that direction.

00:35:37.610 --> 00:35:39.520
My working hypothesis is that

00:35:39.520 --> 00:35:42.150
both poetic and natural
scientific depictions

00:35:42.150 --> 00:35:43.240
of things like disorder

00:35:43.240 --> 00:35:44.073
and chaos

00:35:44.073 --> 00:35:45.230
form a closed loop,

00:35:45.230 --> 00:35:46.810
each ramifying each other

00:35:46.810 --> 00:35:48.910
through a diversity of different channels.

00:35:50.070 --> 00:35:51.890
Just so you don't go away thinking

00:35:51.890 --> 00:35:53.620
poetry is the only one
getting on the action here,

00:35:53.620 --> 00:35:55.070
here's a similar shape

00:35:55.070 --> 00:35:56.300
different word

00:35:56.300 --> 00:35:57.680
but now actually drama

00:35:57.680 --> 00:35:59.321
seems a lot more interested in quantum

00:35:59.321 --> 00:36:00.983
than either poetry or fiction.

00:36:01.870 --> 00:36:03.223
Likewise with galaxy.

00:36:04.250 --> 00:36:05.520
I don't know what to
say about these things,

00:36:05.520 --> 00:36:07.690
I don't know how to interpret them,

00:36:07.690 --> 00:36:09.440
what I do as a scientist I want to

00:36:09.440 --> 00:36:11.920
cast a certain amount of shade on them

00:36:11.920 --> 00:36:12.930
these are averages

00:36:12.930 --> 00:36:16.380
of course averages we know are
affected by extreme events.

00:36:16.380 --> 00:36:17.213
What we really want to do

00:36:17.213 --> 00:36:18.410
is take a time slice in these

00:36:18.410 --> 00:36:19.350
and ask ourselves

00:36:19.350 --> 00:36:20.340
for every point on here

00:36:20.340 --> 00:36:22.240
what is the distribution of texts,

00:36:22.240 --> 00:36:24.433
the average of which is shown here.

00:36:25.840 --> 00:36:26.680
So here I've done that for you,

00:36:26.680 --> 00:36:28.080
I'm looking at a different word now,

00:36:28.080 --> 00:36:29.480
this is temperature in Fiction

00:36:29.480 --> 00:36:30.880
in the same time period,

00:36:30.880 --> 00:36:33.740
the x-axis here is plotting
the normalized word occurrence

00:36:33.740 --> 00:36:35.610
in the text, the word frequency

00:36:35.610 --> 00:36:36.660
and the y-axis is showing

00:36:36.660 --> 00:36:38.100
how many texts

00:36:38.100 --> 00:36:40.290
talk about temperature at that frequency.

00:36:40.290 --> 00:36:41.643
This is a log-log plot,

00:36:42.640 --> 00:36:45.560
and it looks quite a lot like a power law

00:36:45.560 --> 00:36:47.210
that's super cool.

00:36:47.210 --> 00:36:48.760
Again I don't know what this means

00:36:48.760 --> 00:36:50.780
I also know it's not
tremendously surprising,

00:36:50.780 --> 00:36:53.623
power law show up all over
computational linguistics,

00:36:54.810 --> 00:36:55.790
but it's useful for something

00:36:55.790 --> 00:36:57.400
by measuring the slope of this line

00:36:57.400 --> 00:36:58.900
we get the exponent and the power law.

00:36:58.900 --> 00:37:00.220
In other words we get

00:37:00.220 --> 00:37:01.760
in a certain sense how

00:37:01.760 --> 00:37:05.123
how quickly interest in
these words falls off

00:37:05.123 --> 00:37:08.533
as the function of the corpus.

00:37:09.600 --> 00:37:11.710
We can do this for
different words as well,

00:37:11.710 --> 00:37:13.600
here I'm looking at the word infinite

00:37:13.600 --> 00:37:15.170
you can try in poetry, drama and fiction

00:37:15.170 --> 00:37:17.050
and of course we can do this through time.

00:37:17.050 --> 00:37:19.130
These are no longer fits now,

00:37:19.130 --> 00:37:21.700
now my power law hypothesis
is falling apart.

00:37:21.700 --> 00:37:23.230
Maybe it might work for poetry,

00:37:23.230 --> 00:37:25.710
for drama it's a bit more complicated

00:37:25.710 --> 00:37:28.320
but that in itself is
an interesting point.

00:37:28.320 --> 00:37:29.730
As a physicist you might wanna call this

00:37:29.730 --> 00:37:31.810
something like a power spectrum

00:37:31.810 --> 00:37:33.210
and it's interesting to ask,

00:37:34.210 --> 00:37:35.950
where do words enter these genres?

00:37:35.950 --> 00:37:36.940
Are they coming in

00:37:36.940 --> 00:37:38.920
are they just percolating
in through general usage?

00:37:38.920 --> 00:37:41.630
And in which case we might
expect something like this,

00:37:41.630 --> 00:37:42.930
the fictional case.

00:37:42.930 --> 00:37:44.780
Where they're showing up
in the general language

00:37:44.780 --> 00:37:46.740
and there's showing up into texts

00:37:46.740 --> 00:37:48.450
in a more concentrated fashion.

00:37:48.450 --> 00:37:50.120
Or do they show up maybe already

00:37:50.120 --> 00:37:52.030
like in the drama case here,

00:37:52.030 --> 00:37:55.330
do they just pop in as concentrated bursts

00:37:55.330 --> 00:37:57.663
that then flatten out as time goes on.

00:37:58.570 --> 00:37:59.933
So that's just the snapshot
of some of the question

00:37:59.933 --> 00:38:01.530
that I'm interested in.

00:38:01.530 --> 00:38:02.363
Thank you for your attention,

00:38:02.363 --> 00:38:04.392
I'm very eager to hear your thoughts.

00:38:04.392 --> 00:38:06.642
(applause)

00:38:11.400 --> 00:38:12.680
- Thanks so much to Tyler

00:38:12.680 --> 00:38:15.320
and could now invite
Lisa up to speak about

00:38:15.320 --> 00:38:18.353
Lived Experience as a
Theory of Innovation.

00:38:21.122 --> 00:38:23.872
(light applause)

00:38:34.640 --> 00:38:35.970
- Hi everyone I'm Lisa,

00:38:35.970 --> 00:38:37.450
a junior studying Economics

00:38:37.450 --> 00:38:39.710
and I'm also in the Human Rights Program.

00:38:39.710 --> 00:38:41.870
And this year I've been
working at TSAI CITY

00:38:41.870 --> 00:38:44.720
as a part of the Knowledge
Equity Team to study

00:38:44.720 --> 00:38:47.320
some questions that I wanna
present to you here today.

00:38:47.320 --> 00:38:48.730
So to start off with,

00:38:48.730 --> 00:38:51.100
this is Ikea's refugee shelter

00:38:51.100 --> 00:38:53.640
and it was designed at the
height of the refugee crisis

00:38:53.640 --> 00:38:55.340
bringing together Ikea's approach

00:38:55.340 --> 00:38:57.540
of easy construction and easy assembly

00:38:57.540 --> 00:38:59.100
to the massive need of for housing

00:38:59.100 --> 00:39:00.320
for the millions of refugees

00:39:00.320 --> 00:39:01.890
coming from the Syrian Civil War

00:39:01.890 --> 00:39:03.760
and other global conflicts.

00:39:03.760 --> 00:39:06.334
So naturally this shelter
won numerous awards

00:39:06.334 --> 00:39:08.510
it was praised for being easy to assemble

00:39:08.510 --> 00:39:09.790
with no tools necessary

00:39:09.790 --> 00:39:10.850
for being more durable

00:39:10.850 --> 00:39:13.000
for being stab-proof and more.

00:39:13.000 --> 00:39:15.670
Yet when the refugee shelter
was actually deployed

00:39:15.670 --> 00:39:17.180
it became quickly apparent

00:39:17.180 --> 00:39:20.010
that it was not all that
it was talked up to be.

00:39:20.010 --> 00:39:22.220
So some of the complaints
about the shelter.

00:39:22.220 --> 00:39:23.770
Meager, limited,

00:39:23.770 --> 00:39:25.400
lack of use of local materials,

00:39:25.400 --> 00:39:26.330
no insulation,

00:39:26.330 --> 00:39:28.470
lack of natural light.

00:39:28.470 --> 00:39:30.170
How can it be that a shelter that won

00:39:30.170 --> 00:39:32.740
so many awards for changing people's lives

00:39:32.740 --> 00:39:34.820
actually had all these issues

00:39:34.820 --> 00:39:37.010
and I'm sure that the
designers did their research

00:39:37.010 --> 00:39:37.940
but it's clear to me

00:39:37.940 --> 00:39:39.730
that a true understanding

00:39:39.730 --> 00:39:41.670
of the whole spectrum of local needs

00:39:41.670 --> 00:39:44.450
was not at the center of this design.

00:39:44.450 --> 00:39:46.220
So why then would this refugee shelter

00:39:46.220 --> 00:39:48.010
receive so many awards

00:39:48.010 --> 00:39:50.130
when a refugee shelter like this one

00:39:50.130 --> 00:39:52.780
crafted ingeniously out
of scavenged materials

00:39:52.780 --> 00:39:55.090
by refugees themselves is not applauded

00:39:55.090 --> 00:39:56.860
as similarly innovative.

00:39:56.860 --> 00:39:59.000
Why is it that innovation that derives

00:39:59.000 --> 00:40:00.140
from lived experience

00:40:00.140 --> 00:40:03.560
from actually being a refugee
is not receiving awards?

00:40:03.560 --> 00:40:06.330
Why is it that learned
expertise has a monopoly,

00:40:06.330 --> 00:40:08.850
that learned expertise
is the only expertise

00:40:08.850 --> 00:40:11.143
and that professionals
are the only experts?

00:40:12.150 --> 00:40:13.440
Naturally this is hardly limited

00:40:13.440 --> 00:40:15.610
to the international development space.

00:40:15.610 --> 00:40:16.670
I think too often

00:40:16.670 --> 00:40:18.490
especially in the social sector,

00:40:18.490 --> 00:40:20.870
those in power do not
have lived experience

00:40:20.870 --> 00:40:22.790
with the issues at worked hand.

00:40:22.790 --> 00:40:24.870
And so I wanna study why is that?

00:40:24.870 --> 00:40:25.830
In this semester,

00:40:25.830 --> 00:40:28.900
as part of the Knowledge
Equity initiative at TSAI CITY,

00:40:28.900 --> 00:40:31.520
I've been studying some of
the philosophy behind AI

00:40:31.520 --> 00:40:34.510
to see what it can reveal
about the nature of knowledge

00:40:34.510 --> 00:40:36.760
and specifically I've used AI as a foil

00:40:36.760 --> 00:40:39.363
to study how human brains
value lived experience.

00:40:40.580 --> 00:40:42.341
So first AI naturally

00:40:42.341 --> 00:40:45.210
is intelligence exhibited by a computer

00:40:45.210 --> 00:40:47.650
as opposed to a human or a living being,

00:40:47.650 --> 00:40:49.300
and obviously AI does not have to be

00:40:49.300 --> 00:40:51.220
oriented around human intelligence

00:40:51.220 --> 00:40:52.650
or replicating human intelligence

00:40:52.650 --> 00:40:54.400
but that's the approach I'm taking.

00:40:56.100 --> 00:40:58.470
So fundamentally there
are two components of AI,

00:40:58.470 --> 00:40:59.550
data and algorithms.

00:40:59.550 --> 00:41:01.110
We can think about data as a noun

00:41:01.110 --> 00:41:02.770
and algorithms as a verb.

00:41:02.770 --> 00:41:05.030
Data represents characteristics
within our world

00:41:05.030 --> 00:41:07.260
from personal data which
are markers of our lives

00:41:07.260 --> 00:41:09.540
to group data to machine based data.

00:41:09.540 --> 00:41:11.100
Algorithms are the mechanisms

00:41:11.100 --> 00:41:13.370
by which these characteristics
are transformed

00:41:13.370 --> 00:41:14.430
into something useful.

00:41:14.430 --> 00:41:15.410
And more often than not

00:41:15.410 --> 00:41:17.230
that's something useful as a decision

00:41:17.230 --> 00:41:19.540
but it can also be patterns
that help make decisions

00:41:19.540 --> 00:41:21.180
and heuristics as well.

00:41:21.180 --> 00:41:22.320
For us as humans

00:41:22.320 --> 00:41:24.620
we might think of
algorithms within our brains

00:41:24.620 --> 00:41:26.530
as wisdom or knowledge.

00:41:26.530 --> 00:41:28.910
One of the biggest debates
in artificial intelligence

00:41:28.910 --> 00:41:30.760
is algorithms versus data,

00:41:30.760 --> 00:41:32.440
which is more important for AI?

00:41:32.440 --> 00:41:33.980
Well we might see better algorithms

00:41:33.980 --> 00:41:35.150
as better reasoning skills

00:41:35.150 --> 00:41:37.190
and better critical thinking skills,

00:41:37.190 --> 00:41:39.060
and for humans the equivalent of more data

00:41:39.060 --> 00:41:40.680
might be more conversations had

00:41:40.680 --> 00:41:42.710
or more experiences.

00:41:42.710 --> 00:41:45.660
Of course these two
are intimately related.

00:41:45.660 --> 00:41:47.410
Having more conversation for example,

00:41:47.410 --> 00:41:49.540
leads not only to broader
pool of data points

00:41:49.540 --> 00:41:51.000
in which our brains can draw upon

00:41:51.000 --> 00:41:53.620
but also might improve our
critical thinking skills.

00:41:53.620 --> 00:41:56.020
But it is also important to point out

00:41:56.020 --> 00:41:59.020
that data and algorithms
are not the same thing.

00:41:59.020 --> 00:42:02.340
A lack of good data is not
going to be fully compensated by

00:42:02.340 --> 00:42:03.420
a good algorithm

00:42:03.420 --> 00:42:06.093
and a plethora of data
will not compensate for

00:42:06.093 --> 00:42:08.260
a poor algorithm.

00:42:08.260 --> 00:42:10.400
So another question to think about

00:42:10.400 --> 00:42:12.150
is whether more data is always

00:42:12.150 --> 00:42:14.150
gonna produce better decisions

00:42:14.150 --> 00:42:15.470
and that's not necessarily true

00:42:15.470 --> 00:42:17.100
more data can add noise.

00:42:17.100 --> 00:42:17.933
But what is true

00:42:17.933 --> 00:42:20.927
and I'm quoting the core data
science team had here is that

00:42:20.927 --> 00:42:22.740
"Better data is always better."

00:42:22.740 --> 00:42:25.840
So data that is related to the
issue that we are studying.

00:42:25.840 --> 00:42:27.130
So given this framework,

00:42:27.130 --> 00:42:29.210
where does lived experience fit in?

00:42:29.210 --> 00:42:30.670
Well we can think of lived experience

00:42:30.670 --> 00:42:33.760
as having characteristics
of both data and algorithms.

00:42:33.760 --> 00:42:35.720
It's not data represented in numbers

00:42:35.720 --> 00:42:38.070
which is how many of us are
used to thinking of data

00:42:38.070 --> 00:42:40.730
but its data as a record
of our experiences.

00:42:40.730 --> 00:42:43.400
And lived experience
as data is better data

00:42:43.400 --> 00:42:46.270
because it's related to the
issues that we are studying.

00:42:46.270 --> 00:42:48.360
Lived experiences are also like algorithms

00:42:48.360 --> 00:42:50.360
in that in influences the lens

00:42:50.360 --> 00:42:52.560
through which we process information.

00:42:52.560 --> 00:42:53.900
So given all of this,

00:42:53.900 --> 00:42:56.670
I think what the artificial
intelligence model tells us

00:42:56.670 --> 00:42:58.770
is that if the output of our brains

00:42:58.770 --> 00:43:00.200
is the product at the inputs

00:43:00.200 --> 00:43:01.530
and reasoning skills

00:43:01.530 --> 00:43:03.350
then the ideas that we
are able to generate

00:43:03.350 --> 00:43:06.550
are directly related to
the experiences we've had.

00:43:06.550 --> 00:43:09.860
That's learned expertise
will not have the same realm

00:43:09.860 --> 00:43:11.830
of possible ideas as lived expertise

00:43:11.830 --> 00:43:13.390
and vice versa.

00:43:13.390 --> 00:43:16.410
So an example to bring us
back to the refugee shelters,

00:43:16.410 --> 00:43:19.070
someone who has experienced
the insecurity of statelessness

00:43:19.070 --> 00:43:21.210
is going to have an understanding

00:43:21.210 --> 00:43:22.920
of what it means to be a refugee

00:43:22.920 --> 00:43:24.660
and what refugee needs are

00:43:24.660 --> 00:43:26.410
that is not replicated by someone

00:43:26.410 --> 00:43:28.570
who doesn't have that
same lived experience.

00:43:28.570 --> 00:43:29.760
And so the creativity

00:43:29.760 --> 00:43:31.940
and innovation that lived
experience generates

00:43:31.940 --> 00:43:33.540
is fundamentally different from someone

00:43:33.540 --> 00:43:36.470
who is a professional in the
international development space

00:43:36.470 --> 00:43:38.680
while a Master's Degree in
international development

00:43:38.680 --> 00:43:39.840
might provide an algorithm

00:43:39.840 --> 00:43:41.920
with which we can think
about in statelessness

00:43:41.920 --> 00:43:43.300
and insecurity

00:43:43.300 --> 00:43:44.860
that is never going to replace

00:43:44.860 --> 00:43:46.230
the lack of true understanding

00:43:46.230 --> 00:43:47.740
of what it means to be a refugee

00:43:47.740 --> 00:43:50.690
and the innovation that derives
from that lived experience.

00:43:51.750 --> 00:43:53.120
What all of this is to say

00:43:53.120 --> 00:43:55.780
is that lived experience is innovation

00:43:55.780 --> 00:43:57.320
that our realm of possibilities

00:43:57.320 --> 00:44:00.510
is fundamentally related to
the experiences that we've had

00:44:00.510 --> 00:44:02.580
and it's also not to say
that it's lived expert

00:44:02.580 --> 00:44:04.570
experience versus learned experience

00:44:04.570 --> 00:44:05.680
but rather that each of us

00:44:05.680 --> 00:44:08.300
has a unique combination of
lived and learned experiences

00:44:08.300 --> 00:44:10.750
that influence our
ideas and our creativity

00:44:10.750 --> 00:44:13.950
we just need to be better
about recognizing this fact.

00:44:13.950 --> 00:44:17.420
So then how did out bias against
learned expertise develop

00:44:17.420 --> 00:44:19.440
while also based on our own experiences?

00:44:19.440 --> 00:44:21.400
For a variety of reasons
the world we live in

00:44:21.400 --> 00:44:23.010
values the professional

00:44:23.010 --> 00:44:25.920
but this doesn't mean
that lived experience

00:44:25.920 --> 00:44:27.320
is fundamentally less useful

00:44:27.320 --> 00:44:28.970
it just means that we
have socially constructed

00:44:28.970 --> 00:44:30.770
the meaning around lived experience.

00:44:31.750 --> 00:44:34.420
So finally I want to conclude
with a hopeful example

00:44:34.420 --> 00:44:37.390
of how valuing lived experience
can improve our world

00:44:37.390 --> 00:44:40.500
and also see lived experience
as theory of innovation.

00:44:40.500 --> 00:44:43.020
So this is Pigeonly a company founded

00:44:43.020 --> 00:44:45.270
to facilitate communications
between inmates

00:44:45.270 --> 00:44:46.950
and their loved ones.

00:44:46.950 --> 00:44:49.170
Obviously family connection
and communication

00:44:49.170 --> 00:44:50.820
is a crucial support system

00:44:50.820 --> 00:44:52.800
for rebuilding lives after prison

00:44:52.800 --> 00:44:54.770
and that was a crucial point recognized

00:44:54.770 --> 00:44:56.540
not by someone who has
never been to prison

00:44:56.540 --> 00:44:58.420
but by a former prisoner himself.

00:44:58.420 --> 00:45:00.110
So Frederick Hutson an inmate

00:45:00.110 --> 00:45:02.760
founded this company after
his experience in prison

00:45:02.760 --> 00:45:05.420
and used that experience to found

00:45:05.420 --> 00:45:07.520
as the root of his innovation.

00:45:07.520 --> 00:45:10.470
His understanding of how
communication and prisons work

00:45:10.470 --> 00:45:13.050
combined with his learned experience

00:45:13.050 --> 00:45:15.190
participating in accelerator

00:45:15.190 --> 00:45:17.950
is what allowed this idea to flourish.

00:45:17.950 --> 00:45:18.950
So in conclusion,

00:45:18.950 --> 00:45:21.500
I think this conference is
all about breaking boundaries

00:45:21.500 --> 00:45:23.350
but I also hope how we can build bridges

00:45:23.350 --> 00:45:26.720
and see how AI can help
us value lived experience

00:45:26.720 --> 00:45:28.860
as a theory of innovation.

00:45:28.860 --> 00:45:29.934
Thank you.

00:45:29.934 --> 00:45:32.184
(applause)

00:45:39.891 --> 00:45:41.600
- Thank so much to Lisa for that

00:45:41.600 --> 00:45:44.030
and now I'd like to call
up our final speaker

00:45:44.030 --> 00:45:46.410
Mariel will be talking to us about

00:45:46.410 --> 00:45:48.870
Generative and Variational Choreography

00:45:48.870 --> 00:45:50.023
via Machine Learning.

00:46:09.500 --> 00:46:12.060
- So this work started with a question,

00:46:12.060 --> 00:46:14.960
can I use my technical background to

00:46:14.960 --> 00:46:18.590
extend my choreographic
practice and create new work

00:46:18.590 --> 00:46:20.483
that still feels like my own?

00:46:22.090 --> 00:46:24.370
Or to quote my hero,

00:46:24.370 --> 00:46:26.377
post-modern choreographer Pina Bausch,

00:46:26.377 --> 00:46:28.817
"How can I" well she said,

00:46:28.817 --> 00:46:30.540
"I didn't want to imitate anybody.

00:46:30.540 --> 00:46:32.400
Any movement I knew,
I didn't want to use."

00:46:32.400 --> 00:46:34.090
and so to me that means,

00:46:34.090 --> 00:46:35.620
how do I get out of my own head

00:46:35.620 --> 00:46:36.840
and my own body?

00:46:36.840 --> 00:46:38.540
While still remaining in my body

00:46:38.540 --> 00:46:40.743
in creating new work using this tool.

00:46:42.630 --> 00:46:44.593
And what I would say is,

00:46:45.540 --> 00:46:49.340
this not only requires
freshness and novelty.

00:46:49.340 --> 00:46:52.360
Because at the extreme
of freshness and novelty

00:46:52.360 --> 00:46:55.030
could be abstraction or
something that is not meaningful

00:46:55.030 --> 00:46:56.823
or is alienating to an audience.

00:46:58.150 --> 00:47:00.420
But its also dangerous to
stay within a comfort zone

00:47:00.420 --> 00:47:02.010
of something that feels authentic

00:47:02.010 --> 00:47:04.950
because sometimes that
can just be read as trite

00:47:04.950 --> 00:47:07.050
or not generalizable to an audience,

00:47:07.050 --> 00:47:08.310
so what I'm interested in

00:47:08.310 --> 00:47:10.990
is this intersection of freshness novelty

00:47:10.990 --> 00:47:12.530
and authenticity.

00:47:12.530 --> 00:47:13.920
And in Pina Bausch's case,

00:47:13.920 --> 00:47:15.053
I think this often,

00:47:16.980 --> 00:47:18.193
there should be a video,

00:47:21.690 --> 00:47:24.310
this often creates really
fascinating results

00:47:24.310 --> 00:47:25.210
in choreographies.

00:47:26.791 --> 00:47:29.490
So what I chose to embark on

00:47:29.490 --> 00:47:31.890
is this adventure to
combine machine learning

00:47:31.890 --> 00:47:34.990
to generate choreography
in some interesting ways.

00:47:34.990 --> 00:47:36.670
I thought that as a scientist

00:47:36.670 --> 00:47:37.540
I might have something unique

00:47:37.540 --> 00:47:39.460
to contribute to this conversation.

00:47:39.460 --> 00:47:41.210
As well as a choreographer talking about

00:47:41.210 --> 00:47:43.460
lived experiences and
how this can be useful.

00:47:44.540 --> 00:47:46.730
So this is not me alone,

00:47:46.730 --> 00:47:47.790
although I started on my own

00:47:47.790 --> 00:47:50.150
I quickly found out that collaboration

00:47:50.150 --> 00:47:52.780
is one of the best ways
to advance your project

00:47:52.780 --> 00:47:54.900
so I'm in the physics department here

00:47:54.900 --> 00:47:57.380
as a PhD student in particle physics

00:47:57.380 --> 00:48:00.380
and so I've joined up
with my collaborator Chase

00:48:00.380 --> 00:48:02.560
who's a post-doc in the
physics department as well,

00:48:02.560 --> 00:48:05.663
with Doug who is established here,

00:48:06.690 --> 00:48:08.210
as well as my colleague Ilya

00:48:08.210 --> 00:48:10.610
who is a dancer and
researcher in his own right.

00:48:11.860 --> 00:48:14.380
Some VR/AR artist and some new dancers

00:48:14.380 --> 00:48:15.610
who have been thrown in to the mix.

00:48:15.610 --> 00:48:17.620
So a really interesting group of people

00:48:17.620 --> 00:48:18.673
in the room at once.

00:48:20.150 --> 00:48:22.740
My data collection started at the CCAM,

00:48:22.740 --> 00:48:26.210
the Center for Collaborative
Arts and Media.

00:48:26.210 --> 00:48:28.410
This is in their new motion capture studio

00:48:28.410 --> 00:48:30.880
and I've highlighted these
motion capture cameras

00:48:30.880 --> 00:48:33.210
within the room there are twenty in total.

00:48:33.210 --> 00:48:36.150
And so I suited up

00:48:37.330 --> 00:48:40.900
with these very reflective
sensors on my body.

00:48:40.900 --> 00:48:43.360
So overall the data set
that I was analyzing

00:48:43.360 --> 00:48:46.814
was a series of about 55 points in my body

00:48:46.814 --> 00:48:48.950
in three full dimensions

00:48:48.950 --> 00:48:50.683
with very few gaps in the data.

00:48:51.520 --> 00:48:53.000
At a pretty high frame rate

00:48:53.000 --> 00:48:55.040
for a pretty extended period of time.

00:48:55.040 --> 00:48:57.873
This is me dancing in the space.

00:48:59.140 --> 00:49:01.590
So we didn't throw machine learning

00:49:01.590 --> 00:49:04.140
at this problem arbitrarily.

00:49:04.140 --> 00:49:05.490
Machine learning has been shown to be a

00:49:05.490 --> 00:49:08.150
really effective tool for
understanding very complex

00:49:08.150 --> 00:49:10.400
and non-linear data sets.

00:49:10.400 --> 00:49:12.130
That was proven back in the 90s

00:49:12.130 --> 00:49:14.940
to show that we can use
machine learning to approximate

00:49:14.940 --> 00:49:16.910
any continuous and bounded function

00:49:16.910 --> 00:49:18.613
within certain limits.

00:49:19.640 --> 00:49:21.230
With some caveats to that

00:49:21.230 --> 00:49:22.063
but in general

00:49:22.063 --> 00:49:23.300
we approach neural networks

00:49:23.300 --> 00:49:25.920
because they are this
incredibly powerful tool

00:49:25.920 --> 00:49:28.603
for approximating very
complicated functions.

00:49:30.540 --> 00:49:34.080
And this is a bummer that
these videos aren't showing,

00:49:34.080 --> 00:49:34.913
but

00:49:39.489 --> 00:49:42.989
let me see if I can try this one this way.

00:49:45.042 --> 00:49:46.673
This is connected to WiFi right?

00:49:54.610 --> 00:49:55.443
Okay.

00:49:56.960 --> 00:49:59.461
Yeah I don't know what
to do about this but

00:49:59.461 --> 00:50:03.150
I can illustrate and describe

00:50:03.150 --> 00:50:05.510
but basically I would say that

00:50:05.510 --> 00:50:09.030
something that I've also
found interesting about my

00:50:09.030 --> 00:50:11.170
exploration of neural networks is that

00:50:11.170 --> 00:50:13.490
they are choreographic in themselves,

00:50:13.490 --> 00:50:16.200
the training of a neural network involves

00:50:16.200 --> 00:50:17.530
directing

00:50:17.530 --> 00:50:19.710
your the movement of a

00:50:19.710 --> 00:50:22.510
various elements of your
neural network itself

00:50:22.510 --> 00:50:23.620
through this landscape

00:50:23.620 --> 00:50:26.860
this higher dimensional or
lower dimensional landscape

00:50:26.860 --> 00:50:28.530
that represents the error

00:50:28.530 --> 00:50:31.460
inherent in your model
at any one point in time.

00:50:31.460 --> 00:50:32.920
So by

00:50:34.310 --> 00:50:36.520
so by directing our model in its learning

00:50:36.520 --> 00:50:37.353
its

00:50:38.580 --> 00:50:40.023
Yes! Oh great.

00:50:42.760 --> 00:50:43.593
Cool so

00:50:44.500 --> 00:50:47.900
this sort of represents this
trajectory through space.

00:50:47.900 --> 00:50:50.360
Represents this higher dimensional

00:50:51.330 --> 00:50:53.980
or maybe an abstract
representation of my model

00:50:53.980 --> 00:50:55.420
at any one point in time.

00:50:55.420 --> 00:50:57.240
I'm trying to give it
the right instructions

00:50:57.240 --> 00:50:59.780
to get to a minimum of
one of these points.

00:50:59.780 --> 00:51:00.960
So you can see that this model

00:51:00.960 --> 00:51:02.700
is sort of moving towards a minimum.

00:51:02.700 --> 00:51:05.630
So I wanna minimize my
error overall in my model

00:51:05.630 --> 00:51:06.463
and so this is it

00:51:06.463 --> 00:51:08.970
a sense of choreography
in itself of figuring out

00:51:08.970 --> 00:51:11.773
the right ways to direct this model.

00:51:12.980 --> 00:51:14.840
And so I wanted to emphasize this

00:51:18.740 --> 00:51:22.070
because I already know
of choreographic research

00:51:22.070 --> 00:51:24.440
as very rigorous research
in its own right.

00:51:24.440 --> 00:51:26.940
So I didn't want to approach this problem

00:51:27.990 --> 00:51:31.100
as a sense of elevating choreographic work

00:51:31.100 --> 00:51:33.050
through a lens of technology.

00:51:33.050 --> 00:51:35.890
So in a sense I'm seeing
this from the perspective as

00:51:36.820 --> 00:51:38.850
a deep dive into machine learning research

00:51:38.850 --> 00:51:41.040
with an interesting and meaningful dataset

00:51:41.040 --> 00:51:44.250
as well as perhaps enhancing
my choreographic research

00:51:44.250 --> 00:51:45.790
through an extended body

00:51:45.790 --> 00:51:47.750
or an extended choreographic mind.

00:51:47.750 --> 00:51:50.490
And I wanna highlight three
areas of this research

00:51:50.490 --> 00:51:52.970
that this work has evolved into.

00:51:52.970 --> 00:51:53.870
Preservation

00:51:53.870 --> 00:51:54.703
Variation

00:51:54.703 --> 00:51:55.683
and invention.

00:51:56.590 --> 00:51:58.470
By preservation I mean a sort of

00:51:58.470 --> 00:52:00.570
physicalized choreographic brain

00:52:00.570 --> 00:52:02.430
and for me that has a lot of meaning

00:52:02.430 --> 00:52:04.250
that I don't have time to get into.

00:52:04.250 --> 00:52:06.000
But I'll sketch out that

00:52:06.000 --> 00:52:08.170
to me movement is a part of my identity

00:52:08.170 --> 00:52:10.590
and I don't have a strong
way of preserving that

00:52:10.590 --> 00:52:11.783
for my own life.

00:52:12.890 --> 00:52:17.180
And so this idea of
preserving someone's style

00:52:17.180 --> 00:52:18.760
like for me people have often said that

00:52:18.760 --> 00:52:20.230
something about my movement

00:52:20.230 --> 00:52:22.010
reminds them of my mom

00:52:22.010 --> 00:52:23.700
so there's a sense of

00:52:23.700 --> 00:52:25.660
what does it mean to preserve a human?

00:52:25.660 --> 00:52:28.853
When style and movement is
important to your own identity.

00:52:30.100 --> 00:52:32.640
And also for myself to retain
the potential to invent

00:52:32.640 --> 00:52:34.670
with my current choreographic body

00:52:34.670 --> 00:52:37.870
or maybe future iterations
of my choreographic body

00:52:37.870 --> 00:52:39.010
at any point in time.

00:52:39.010 --> 00:52:40.750
So I find that very fascinating.

00:52:40.750 --> 00:52:43.360
And so this literally can represent

00:52:43.360 --> 00:52:46.660
the actual data of
motion capture of myself

00:52:46.660 --> 00:52:47.970
improving in the studio

00:52:47.970 --> 00:52:50.320
which is compelling in its own right.

00:52:50.320 --> 00:52:54.400
But I hope to extend that by also

00:52:54.400 --> 00:52:57.830
physicalizing the potential
of my body to choreograph.

00:52:57.830 --> 00:52:59.240
So not only a record

00:52:59.240 --> 00:53:02.760
but a kind of limitless model

00:53:02.760 --> 00:53:04.660
that can constantly generate new work.

00:53:05.520 --> 00:53:06.900
Now I wanted to show what that looks like

00:53:06.900 --> 00:53:08.680
when the model is not trained very well.

00:53:08.680 --> 00:53:09.957
Just to give you guys a sense

00:53:09.957 --> 00:53:12.371
that's it's very difficult to

00:53:12.371 --> 00:53:13.204
(audience laughs)

00:53:13.204 --> 00:53:14.660
to capture a human body moving

00:53:14.660 --> 00:53:16.520
it's a complicated problem

00:53:16.520 --> 00:53:19.350
and I am not imposing any
constraints into this model

00:53:19.350 --> 00:53:21.720
about what a human should look like and so

00:53:21.720 --> 00:53:22.900
I hope that gives some context

00:53:22.900 --> 00:53:25.060
for some of the exciting results

00:53:25.060 --> 00:53:26.700
that we were able to achieve later

00:53:26.700 --> 00:53:27.930
where we are able to generate

00:53:27.930 --> 00:53:29.910
arbitrarily like sequences of movement

00:53:29.910 --> 00:53:32.270
that really do feel
authentically like myself

00:53:32.270 --> 00:53:34.220
because it is derived from my own data.

00:53:36.090 --> 00:53:39.170
Now not only are we
interested in preservation

00:53:39.170 --> 00:53:40.750
we're interested in variation.

00:53:40.750 --> 00:53:43.070
So preservation might
be looking only towards

00:53:43.070 --> 00:53:44.350
future results.

00:53:44.350 --> 00:53:46.250
Variation is rather looking inward

00:53:46.250 --> 00:53:47.840
trying to understand something deeper

00:53:47.840 --> 00:53:49.680
about what my choreography represents

00:53:49.680 --> 00:53:52.490
and so to me that has represented

00:53:52.490 --> 00:53:55.660
projecting this complicated
dimensional data set

00:53:55.660 --> 00:53:57.780
into a reduced dimensional data set

00:53:57.780 --> 00:54:01.203
to understand some basic
features about how it operates.

00:54:02.130 --> 00:54:03.760
As a dancer this is also interesting

00:54:03.760 --> 00:54:05.900
because to me its stripping the body away

00:54:05.900 --> 00:54:07.430
from the choreographic work

00:54:07.430 --> 00:54:10.280
and focusing in on some
kind of innate idea

00:54:10.280 --> 00:54:13.340
that is not disturbed by the body

00:54:13.340 --> 00:54:15.553
and whatever imperfections it might have.

00:54:16.560 --> 00:54:19.540
And it also allows me to
explore related movements

00:54:19.540 --> 00:54:23.390
to a input datasets that
I've already included

00:54:23.390 --> 00:54:25.010
so this is great for a rehearsal studio

00:54:25.010 --> 00:54:30.010
this idea of riffing off a
choreographic idea many times.

00:54:30.060 --> 00:54:34.010
So this is one example of how
we can look at choreography

00:54:34.010 --> 00:54:35.480
in an abstract way.

00:54:35.480 --> 00:54:38.216
This is using principal component analysis

00:54:38.216 --> 00:54:39.910
and what this does is

00:54:39.910 --> 00:54:44.370
this plot shows a bit of
my choreography over time.

00:54:44.370 --> 00:54:46.860
But of course it doesn't
look like choreography

00:54:46.860 --> 00:54:48.380
I could peer into this model

00:54:48.380 --> 00:54:50.070
at various points

00:54:50.070 --> 00:54:51.230
so what I've illustrated here

00:54:51.230 --> 00:54:54.220
are two points that are separate
in time in the sequences

00:54:54.220 --> 00:54:56.540
that have been somehow grouped
together by this model.

00:54:56.540 --> 00:54:58.660
You can see that the model
is capturing something

00:54:58.660 --> 00:55:01.270
that feels real or intrinsic
without the choreographic idea

00:55:01.270 --> 00:55:03.250
something about moving upwards for example

00:55:03.250 --> 00:55:05.800
or me crawling on all fours.

00:55:05.800 --> 00:55:09.940
So there are some ideas that
are grouped in a meaningful way

00:55:09.940 --> 00:55:12.500
We also have some more powerful ways of

00:55:12.500 --> 00:55:14.740
reducing the dimensionality
of this data set

00:55:14.740 --> 00:55:16.562
PCA is actually a fairly simple

00:55:16.562 --> 00:55:20.630
way of looking at data
because it is linear.

00:55:20.630 --> 00:55:22.430
Now machine learning gives us non-linear

00:55:22.430 --> 00:55:23.670
learning capabilities

00:55:23.670 --> 00:55:27.760
so what we ended up using
was we call an auto-encoder

00:55:27.760 --> 00:55:30.170
which can represent a lower dimensional

00:55:30.170 --> 00:55:31.430
representation of this work

00:55:31.430 --> 00:55:33.443
in a non-linear complicated way.

00:55:34.760 --> 00:55:39.226
Here are some examples of
this abstract, dimensional

00:55:39.226 --> 00:55:41.230
version of my work.

00:55:41.230 --> 00:55:45.350
This represents a variation
on a reference sequence A

00:55:46.320 --> 00:55:48.210
and so reference sequence B here

00:55:48.210 --> 00:55:51.520
is somehow found by this
model to be highly related

00:55:51.520 --> 00:55:52.640
but somehow different

00:55:52.640 --> 00:55:55.500
and also completely generated in a sense.

00:55:55.500 --> 00:55:58.900
So I'll flash some quick
examples variation.

00:55:58.900 --> 00:56:02.590
So here what this
represents the red person

00:56:02.590 --> 00:56:04.380
is close to real data,

00:56:04.380 --> 00:56:07.760
whereas the colorful person is a

00:56:07.760 --> 00:56:09.063
generated variation.

00:56:10.386 --> 00:56:11.270
What's interesting about this

00:56:11.270 --> 00:56:12.880
is that it's able to maintain

00:56:12.880 --> 00:56:15.030
a similar sense of rhythm and style

00:56:15.030 --> 00:56:17.610
and yet it somehow feels completely fresh

00:56:17.610 --> 00:56:21.010
and is giving it's own
perspective on this idea.

00:56:21.010 --> 00:56:23.630
So in some cases we see that

00:56:24.560 --> 00:56:26.350
it adds spins or rotation

00:56:26.350 --> 00:56:28.640
sometimes it removes spins and rotations

00:56:28.640 --> 00:56:30.880
or this is my favorite example,

00:56:30.880 --> 00:56:34.370
translates a kick into an
upward movement in the arms.

00:56:34.370 --> 00:56:36.300
So there's a sense of
upwardness as an idea

00:56:36.300 --> 00:56:38.550
translated into a
different part of the body.

00:56:39.440 --> 00:56:41.040
And finally invention,

00:56:41.040 --> 00:56:42.760
breaking new choreographic ground

00:56:42.760 --> 00:56:44.310
the I otherwise didn't have access to

00:56:44.310 --> 00:56:45.673
without this technology.

00:56:46.680 --> 00:56:49.130
This allows us to explore new areas

00:56:49.130 --> 00:56:51.430
of my choreographic latent space.

00:56:51.430 --> 00:56:52.520
That I as a human

00:56:52.520 --> 00:56:55.070
have not been able to force
myself to access through

00:56:55.070 --> 00:56:58.420
whatever gains and choreographic
constraints I want to use.

00:56:58.420 --> 00:57:01.110
So here I'm gonna show
some generated sequences

00:57:01.110 --> 00:57:03.830
from my choreographic latent space

00:57:03.830 --> 00:57:06.300
that the model has constructed for me.

00:57:06.300 --> 00:57:08.310
They're quite short so I'll
flash through some of them,

00:57:08.310 --> 00:57:11.450
but you can see that
they look like a person

00:57:11.450 --> 00:57:13.003
and to me it feels like me,

00:57:14.670 --> 00:57:16.323
but they're completely invented.

00:57:20.207 --> 00:57:22.400
And finally I would say

00:57:22.400 --> 00:57:24.940
this has just been the
start of some of our

00:57:24.940 --> 00:57:26.280
play and our research,

00:57:26.280 --> 00:57:27.930
there's a lot of data to be scraped

00:57:27.930 --> 00:57:29.520
also from the video performances

00:57:29.520 --> 00:57:31.380
and so I've shown
examples of this on myself

00:57:31.380 --> 00:57:32.710
in a rehearsal studio.

00:57:32.710 --> 00:57:33.720
What this allows me to do

00:57:33.720 --> 00:57:35.820
is to take choreographers or dancers

00:57:35.820 --> 00:57:39.550
who I couldn't put into the
Yale CCAM Motion Capture Studio

00:57:39.550 --> 00:57:41.670
and instead look at videos
of their performances

00:57:41.670 --> 00:57:43.660
and analyze their movements in that way.

00:57:43.660 --> 00:57:45.990
So I can imagine sampling
from a combination

00:57:45.990 --> 00:57:48.393
of many choreographers
latent spaces at once.

00:57:49.520 --> 00:57:51.250
We're also interested
in translating motion

00:57:51.250 --> 00:57:52.770
from between bodies

00:57:52.770 --> 00:57:54.210
and also

00:57:54.210 --> 00:57:55.830
analyzing duets and partner work

00:57:55.830 --> 00:57:57.380
to imagine generating a partner

00:57:57.380 --> 00:57:59.710
given an input sequence of a single mover.

00:57:59.710 --> 00:58:01.950
And so on and so on,

00:58:01.950 --> 00:58:03.650
research doesn't have an end

00:58:03.650 --> 00:58:04.563
so thank you.

00:58:06.022 --> 00:58:08.272
(applause)

00:58:13.578 --> 00:58:15.246
- Well thank you Mariel

00:58:15.246 --> 00:58:16.950
and thanks to all of our presenters

00:58:16.950 --> 00:58:19.750
if I could just ask for
a round of applause for

00:58:19.750 --> 00:58:23.914
Zen, Frances, Amanda, Keniel,
Tyler, Lisa and Mariel.

00:58:23.914 --> 00:58:25.414
Thank you so much.

